    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>The kitchen is often described as the heart of the home, but its countertops (or worktops, as we usually call them in the UK) are its most valuable real estate. While their primary purpose is practical, providing a surface for meal preparation and organisation, they also offer a real opportunity for interior expression. Mastering worktop styling involves a delicate balance: you want the space to feel curated and intentional without sacrificing the workspace you need for daily life.</p>
<p>Good styling turns a cold, bare surface into a warm, inviting vignette. It is about more than just placing objects; it is about scale, texture, colour and the “rule of three”. By choosing items that serve both a practical and an aesthetic purpose, you can lift your kitchen from a standard cooking area to a designer-level showcase.</p>
<!--figure:open-kitchen-marble-island|An open-plan kitchen with a marble island, bar stools and pendant lights, where a largely clear worktop lets the stone take centre stage.-->

<h2 id="layered-boards">1. Layered boards</h2>
<!--figure:white-handleless-kitchen|A white handleless kitchen with a marble splashback, island and upholstered bar stools: a calm backdrop where a few leaning boards would add warmth.-->
<p>One of the most effective ways to add height and texture to a kitchen corner is by layering chopping boards. Instead of tucking your boards away in a cupboard, lean them against the splashback. This does two jobs: it keeps the boards within easy reach for food prep and introduces a natural, organic element to the room.</p>
<p>When layering, vary the shapes and sizes. Mix a large rectangular wooden board with a smaller round marble board. The contrast between warm wood and cool stone adds visual interest. A vertical arrangement like this can also help disguise sockets or minor imperfections in the splashback, creating a relaxed, lived-in designer look.</p>

<h2 id="versatile-trays">2. Versatile trays</h2>
<p>Trays are a favourite tool of interior designers. They act as “anchors” for smaller items that might otherwise look like clutter. By grouping oils, vinegars and salt cellars on a tray, you define a specific zone for cooking essentials. This keeps everything organised and makes cleaning easier, as you only need to lift the tray to wipe the worktop beneath.</p>
<p>Choose a tray material that contrasts with your worktop. If you have dark granite, a light oak or woven rattan tray can provide much-needed warmth. If you have white quartz, a dark metal or stone tray adds a sophisticated edge.</p>

<h2 id="live-greenery">3. Live greenery</h2>
<p>No kitchen feels complete without a touch of nature. Greenery softens the hard lines of cabinetry and appliances, bringing life and colour into the space. Fresh herbs are the gold standard for kitchen greenery: they are attractive, fragrant and useful for cooking.</p>
<p>If you don’t have green fingers, a large vase of seasonal branches, such as olive stems, eucalyptus or cherry blossom, can create a dramatic focal point. For a more permanent solution, good-quality “real-touch” faux plants offer the same look without the maintenance. The key is to choose plants that feel appropriate for a culinary setting, and if you have pets, check that anything you bring in is safe for them.</p>

<h2 id="ceramic-canisters">4. Ceramic canisters</h2>
<p>Storage can be a design statement. Instead of keeping flour, sugar or coffee beans in their original packaging, decant them into attractive ceramic or glass canisters. This creates a uniform look that feels intentional and high-end.</p>
<p>Canisters are also an easy way to introduce a new texture. Matte ceramics feel soft and modern, while fluted glass or hammered metal adds a vintage or industrial touch. Choose canisters with airtight seals to help keep ingredients fresh, so style and substance work together.</p>

<h2 id="statement-art">5. Statement art</h2>
<p>Art is usually reserved for the living room or hallway, but a small framed piece on the kitchen worktop can make the space feel surprisingly sophisticated. It shifts the kitchen from a “work zone” to an extension of the home’s living space.</p>
<p>Select art that can cope with the kitchen environment. Oil paintings, original or printed, work well because they add a sense of history and depth. Avoid placing valuable or fragile pieces near the sink or hob, where they might be exposed to moisture, heat or grease. A small easel can also be used to prop up art, adding an extra layer of dimension to your styling.</p>

<h2 id="statement-appliances">6. Statement appliances</h2>
<p>Appliances are often the largest items on the worktop, so it pays to choose them with looks in mind. Well-made appliances in timeless finishes, such as matte black, brushed steel or cream, can act as functional sculptures.</p>
<p>If you own a beautiful stand mixer or espresso machine, let it be a focal point rather than hiding it in a cupboard. To keep the look curated, avoid overcrowding: keep the area around a statement appliance relatively clear so it has room to “breathe” and stands out as a design element.</p>

<h2 id="fruit-displays">7. Fruit displays</h2>
<p>Fruit is one of the most affordable and effective styling tools. A bowl of fresh produce adds an instant pop of colour and a sense of abundance. Lemons, limes, artichokes and green apples are popular choices because they keep well and provide a consistent, vibrant hue.</p>
<p>The vessel matters as much as the fruit. A pedestal bowl (or compote) adds height, while a wide wooden dough bowl gives a rustic, farmhouse feel. It is also a simple way to change the look of your kitchen with the seasons: bright citrus in summer, deep red pomegranates in winter.</p>

<h2 id="scented-accents">8. Scented accents</h2>
<p>Kitchens are sensory spaces, and while the smell of baking bread is hard to beat, a good candle or diffuser can lift the atmosphere when the hob is off. Choose scents that suit a kitchen, such as citrus, herbs or fresh linen notes, and avoid heavy florals that might clash with food aromas.</p>
<p>Styling a candle involves more than just putting it on the worktop. Set it on a small marble coaster to give it importance, and add an attractive box of matches or a wick trimmer alongside to create a complete “moment”. Never leave a lit candle unattended, and keep it well away from tea towels, cupboards and anything else that could catch.</p>

<h2 id="culinary-literature">9. Culinary literature</h2>
<p>Cookbooks are not just for recipes; they are beautiful objects in their own right. A stack of your favourite cookbooks adds height and a personal touch to the worktop. They hint at your interests and give guests something to browse while you cook.</p>
<p>Look for books with attractive spines or covers. Stack them horizontally to create a platform for a small bowl or candle, or line them up vertically between two heavy bookends. This adds a “library” feel to the kitchen, making it feel more like a curated, lived-in room. Keep them away from the sink and hob, where splashes and steam can damage paper.</p>
<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Before you add any decorative piece, clear the worktop completely and mark out your main prep zone, ideally at least 60 cm (24 in) of uninterrupted surface. Style only around it, and the kitchen will stay as practical as it is pretty.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="groomed-sinks">10. Groomed sinks</h2>
<p>The area around the sink is often the most cluttered part of the kitchen, but it doesn’t have to be. Replace mismatched plastic soap bottles with a coordinated set of glass or ceramic dispensers. This simple swap instantly upgrades the look of your “wet zone”.</p>
<p>To keep the area tidy, place the dispensers on a small tray or a wooden soap stand. Lifting them off the worktop stops water pooling underneath and creates a more intentional look. A good dish brush with a wooden handle completes the scene, turning a chore-heavy area into a styled vignette.</p>

